{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>When you go out to war, etc.</b> It may be that Scripture alludes to a person's war with his evil inclination, and it comes to remove faintness from his heart, saying <b>when you go out to war</b> — the war known to be greater than which there is none. And behold, in this war there are two things difficult for a person that exhaust his strength: first, that he is not trained in warfare as he is trained and accustomed to this species of war, that of his evil inclination; second, that the person's <em>mounting</em> of the words of the tempter in all that it says to him regarding forbidden matters — to steal, to rob, to pride himself, and to eat whatever his soul craves — and this prevents him from hearing the words of Torah and the commandments. How much more so after he has stumbled in many transgressions, when the evil forces that were born from his evil deeds multiply, as I have explained in other places, so that he is among them like a barren tree that cannot be turned back." "<b>For this reason</b> the word of HaShem comes here and says: <b>when you go out to war and you see</b> with the eyes of your intellect <b>horse and chariot</b> — \"horse\" corresponding to the fact that the evil inclination is a horse prepared for battle, which is not so for a person; \"chariot\" corresponding to the person's being mounted and drawn and craving after things. And His saying <b>a people greater than you</b> corresponds to the evil forces that multiplied from him on account of his evil deeds, as we have said. Even so, His good word comes and says: <b>do not fear them</b>, and the reason is <b>for HaShem your Elohim is with you</b> — meaning, it is true that if you were coming to this war in your own strength you would have no strength to stand in this war; but since HaShem your Elohim is with you, He has great power to deliver you, for when a person comes to purify himself, the Holy One, blessed be He, receives him and he cleaves to HaShem, and He subdues his adversaries." "<b>And He concludes</b> by saying <b>who brought you up from the land of Egypt</b>, for this is a decisive sign that HaShem strips the kelipah from the holiness and refines it, and this is the sod of the elevation of Israel from Egypt, as is known to those who know grace. And this is for you a sign so that you may strengthen yourself in the war. And so it says (Psalms 37:32): ״צוֹפֶה רָשָׁע לַצַּדִּיק וְגוֹ׳ ה׳ לֹא יַעַזְבֶנּוּ בְיָדוֹ״, and they said, their memory for blessing (Kiddushin 30b), that the verse speaks of the evil inclination, which overpowers a person, while HaShem helps him and saves him from its hand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The angle-bracketed word <חובקת> appears to be a variant or scribal insertion for 'embracing/clinging'; rendered contextually as 'mounting' in the sense of the person being mounted/overwhelmed by the tempter's words. The closing simile כחררה שאין לה הופכין is obscure; rendered hyper-literally as 'like a barren tree that cannot be turned back,' though the precise meaning of חררה in this context is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:20.749032151Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}